@startuml NeuralBrowser_ReActActivity
title NeuralBrowser — AI Agent ReAct Loop Activity Diagram

skinparam {
  BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  BorderColor #000000
  FontColor #000000
  FontName JetBrains Mono
  FontSize 12
  ArrowColor #000000
  ArrowThickness 1.2
  Shadowing false
  RoundCorner 0
  DefaultTextAlignment center
  Padding 8
  Margin 6
  LineThickness 1
  Monochrome true
  LineType ortho
}

skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  BorderColor #000000
  FontColor #000000
  BarColor #000000
  StartColor #000000
  EndColor #000000
}

start

:Receive Autonomous Browsing Task;
:Initialize Step Count = 0;
:Compile ReAct System Prompt with Tools Schema;

repeat
  :Extract active web page DOM structure;
  :Add DOM Context to Prompt Message History;
  :Spawn AgentWorker Thread;
  :Request Next Action from Local LLM (Ollama);
  :Receive Response String;
  
  if (Is response valid JSON?) then (yes)
    :Parse JSON Object;
    :Extract "tool" and "params";
    
    if (Is tool == "answer"?) then (yes)
      :Extract final response message;
      :Update BrowserWindow Chat UI;
      :Clean up background threads;
      stop
    else (no)
      :Pass action parameters to ActionExecutor;
      :Simulate action (Click, Fill, Navigate, or Scroll) via JS;
      :Log Action Result to Message History;
      :Increment Step Count;
    endif
    
  else (no)
    :Log JSON parsing exception;
    :Append error helper to LLM context history;
    :Increment Step Count;
  endif
  
backward:Check Loop Safety Limits;
repeat while (Is Step Count < 10?) is (yes) not (no)

:Forced stop at safety limit (Max 10 steps reached);
:Display exceeded steps error message to user UI;
:Clean up worker resources;

stop

note right
  The ReAct loop enforces strict structured outputs. 
  If the LLM generates plain conversational text instead of JSON, 
  the parsing check logs it as an observation and asks it to correct.
end note

note left
  A strict safety cap of 10 loops prevents local models 
  from entering recursive navigation loops or infinite loops, 
  avoiding memory leak cycles.
end note

@enduml
